WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.268
Secrets should be accessed through application identity, not through broad
human credentials whenever possible.

2
00:00:06.268 --> 00:00:16.567
A Python service, scheduled job, data pipeline, or background worker
should have a clear service account, workload identity, or runtime
identity that can be granted only the access it needs.

3
00:00:16.567 --> 00:00:22.707
This makes secret access reviewable and limits the blast radius if one
workload is misconfigured or compromised.

4
00:00:22.707 --> 00:00:28.847
Least privilege means each application or workload receives only the
secrets required for its approved purpose.

5
00:00:28.847 --> 00:00:31.982
Avoid broad wildcard access to entire secret namespaces.

6
00:00:31.982 --> 00:00:37.802
A reporting job should not read payment processor credentials if it only
needs a read-only analytics token.

7
00:00:37.802 --> 00:00:41.640
A staging application should not read production database passwords.

8
00:00:41.640 --> 00:00:48.548
A tenant-specific service should not receive customer secrets outside its
boundary unless the design explicitly requires it.

9
00:00:48.548 --> 00:00:51.363
CI/CD and developer access need special attention.

10
00:00:51.363 --> 00:01:00.765
Build systems may need deployment credentials or secret references, but
they should not automatically expose every production secret to every
branch, job, or contributor.

11
00:01:00.765 --> 00:01:12.471
Repository permissions, branch protections at a high level, approval
workflows, and environment-specific access help prevent secrets from
becoming available in places where code is less trusted or less reviewed.

12
00:01:12.471 --> 00:01:16.500
Least privilege is maintained through review, not set once and forgotten.

13
00:01:16.500 --> 00:01:21.489
Secrets should have owners, purpose, environment, allowed consumers, and
review cadence.

14
00:01:21.489 --> 00:01:25.007
High-impact secrets may need approval before access is granted.

15
00:01:25.007 --> 00:01:27.822
When a service is retired, access should be removed.

16
00:01:27.822 --> 00:01:31.276
When responsibilities change, permissions should be updated.

17
00:01:31.276 --> 00:01:37.800
The application should access only the secrets it needs for the task it
performs, and evidence should support that claim.
